Subject: gl: serve the claimed limit on a backend that enforces nothing

min(claim, driver) is the right rule on a backend that enforces what it
reports, and the wrong one on a backend that does not. The clamp did not
distinguish them, so on the deployment target -- a headless server with no GPU,
where ANGLE runs SwiftShader -- a composed profile's discrete-GPU renderer
string sat above the software rasteriser's own limit table.

That is the cheapest possible contradiction to detect. A page reads
MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE 8192 next to an NVIDIA GeForce string and needs no GL work at
all to call it impossible: every discrete GPU of that generation reports 16384
or more, so it is a lookup table, not a probe. The over-claim the clamp was
avoiding costs a detector a multi-hundred-megabyte allocation at the reported
maximum, which is not something a page does on every load. Trading the free
check for the expensive one is backwards.

Which backends enforce is measured rather than assumed. Through the shipped
152.0.7977.83 macOS artifact, allocating with texStorage2D / texStorage3D /
renderbufferStorageMultisample and then checking framebuffer completeness,
clearing to green and reading a pixel back:

  ANGLE/Metal        reports MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE 16384, MAX_3D_TEXTURE_SIZE 2048,
                     MAX_SAMPLES 4. 8192 and 16384 both allocate, are
                     framebuffer-complete and read back green. 32768 returns
                     GL_INVALID_VALUE, its framebuffer is incomplete and the
                     clear and read return GL_INVALID_FRAMEBUFFER_OPERATION. A
                     3D texture at 4096 returns GL_INVALID_VALUE. 8x
                     multisample returns GL_INVALID_OPERATION. Every reported
                     number is a real ceiling and every one is usable up to it.

  ANGLE/SwiftShader  nothing errors, at any size, on any of the three. A 3D
                     texture eight times the reported maximum is accepted
                     without a GL error. The numbers are soft constants, not
                     hardware ceilings -- which patch 0027 established from the
                     other direction when it raised three of them by editing
                     OUTLINE_RESOLUTION.

So the hook takes the claim where the backend enforces nothing and keeps min()
where it does. The test is the ANGLE implementation, and it names SwiftShader
specifically rather than using IsSoftwareGLImplementation(), because that also
covers D3D11 WARP and WARP's enforcement has not been measured here. Guessing
in the permissive direction is precisely how an over-claim turns into a failing
draw call.

All three query paths change together, because a limit read through
glGetInteger64v or glGetFloatv is the same claim read through a different
entry point. MAX_ELEMENT_INDEX is the case that moves on the 64-bit path: the
three hardware anchors claim 4294967294 where SwiftShader reports 1073741823,
and an index buffer is validated against the reported number rather than
against the rasteriser.

The residual is real and is not being softened. On a software backend a page
that allocates at exactly the claimed maximum gets a framebuffer that is not
complete and reads back black, where a real device returns what it cleared to.
Two things about it. It is the backend's, not the claim's: measured above, a
16384 texture already fails completeness under SwiftShader, and 16384 is what
this fork already reports there -- patch 0027 raised it from 8192, so the defect
predates this patch by seventy-five patches and is not introduced here. And the
reference product has it identically: measured on a live headless Linux box, it
reports MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE 16384 and fails texStorage2D at 16384, where stock
Chrome reports 8192 and fails only above 8192.

Where this fork can be better than the reference, and at no cost, is
completeness of the table rather than the size of any one number. The reference
serves MAX_3D_TEXTURE_SIZE 2048 -- SwiftShader's own value -- behind a GeForce
RTX 3070 claim, which is a second lookup-table contradiction needing no
allocation at all. Serving the anchor's measured capture verbatim, every field,
leaves a reported table that matches a real measured device on every field, so
the cheap checks all pass and only the expensive allocation probe fails. That is
why the claim is served whole rather than field by field: a partially served
table is exactly what makes the reference's 2048 detectable.

Nothing changes on a hardware host. BackendEnforcesGlLimits() is true there, so
the code that runs is the code that ran before, min() included, warning
included.

Compile-unverified at the time of writing; V1 is the gate this is submitted to.

Ledger: webgl.limits, gpu.angle-backend, headless.gpu-stack.
--- a/ui/gl/gl_gl_api_implementation.cc
+++ b/ui/gl/gl_gl_api_implementation.cc
